u wanted them to be final bosses, and the Chronicles of Darkness doesn't really concern itself much with balancing splats against each other anyway (if they did then Mages wouldn't be able to eradicate someone with 5 dots in an Arcanum while also being able to make the Spell not Withstood using an exceptional success), so there isn't really much of a reason to not make them really powerful, given the idea that they are supposed to be attacked often and also do things that invite attack often.<br /> <br /> Another idea that could help with the final boss aesthetic is to have it so that at certain Dark Power ratings a Dark Master gains additional transformations, allowing for more powerful ones to have more than two &quot;stages&quot; to their battle, representing more dangerous bosses. Something that may help with the idea of making each Dark Master unique is to give Deathbed Transformations less specific powers; instead of &quot;Extra Legs&quot; which grant a bonus to speed, they can have &quot;Enhanced Speed&quot; which grants them additional Speed and then presents a number of suggested features that could provide that bonus, such as extra legs, the ability to simply run faster, a kind of dark engine that propels the Dark Master forward with a dark shadow trail left behind as they move, and so on. This could help a lot when designing unique forms.<br /> <br /> Personally I would tone down the apparent fear of Mages in the stereotype sections, as it kind of goes against the idea of their arrogance and power, and it is also generally absent for Leviathans, many of whom would likely be more terrifying and powerful than the majority of Mages because they gain power earlier by just having access to Apotheosis and the high level powers that come with it. In particular, if a Dark Master truly had a nuke that they had stolen from the government, a Mage wouldn't be any more of a threat in the face of a nuke than a Leviathan. As well, given the idea that they are supposed to be final bosses, they probably should be able to take on Mages (as well as Leviathans, for that matter) as well, in addition to being confident that they can.<br /> <br /> As far as the Gods go, I would once again recommend giving them more unique systems as opposed to using the systems from Hunter, as it would make them seem more distinct and important to the game (and could also allow for making them more powerful and threatening), and also making them less likely to get directly involved, instead sending primarily their servants. If a conflict with a God does occur, they should be extremely powerful but able to be beaten, and there should be great benefits to be had from doing so. Basically, anything that should be important to the game (Minions, Dungeons, Gods) should likely have their own systems instead of directly using ones from other gamelines, as it will make them seem more important and unique. Another good step would be fleshing out the society and culture of the Gods and making them seem less uniform and more unique. For one thing, the idea of them all getting together in a meeting and deciding that humanity should worship them for nothing would probably be better if reference was made to a council of their leaders coming to the decision, as it once again makes them seem less homogeneous. Also, an extremely minor adjustment, I would suggest replacing the reference to spirits of Rank 4 and 5 in the Gods section with spirits of Rank 6 and higher, as they are the entities more commonly described as being god-like in nature.<br /> <br /> Reading some of the other posts, I personally find the idea of them being a sub-template far less interesting, and I think it's fine to have them as a full template as long as some things are fleshed out (Minions, Deathbed Transformations, Dungeons, as well as the Gods and some of the Dark Master abilities and powers.) Overall, I am quite interested in this. I don't have much experience with sub-templates, so I can't help